  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 89f883f643068e341b758c2e9fd6b2c9, type: 3}
  m_Name: PrototypeChapter
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::Encumbrance.Journal.JournalChapterData
  sentences:
  - W
  - WASD
  - ABCDEFG
  - AHA
  - Tree
  - Rock
  - Sky
  - Sun
  - Fascinating
  - Merely handed layers of parchment and a quill,
  - and I'm subsequently reduced to the awe of a child.
  - My hand glides across,
  - met with the resistance of the pages against the sharp edge.
  - It's not much, it's almost therapeutic.
  - Though even in reward,
  - it's difficult to pretend that this is not some form of mockery by the gods.
  - But very well, I shall shoulder this 'reward' alongside my assignment.
  - Begin ascent.
  - The sun seeps through the strands of my cloak,
  - gently against the skin I dare not shed.
  - I expected not a 'gift', less so one that took this form.
  - Is it normal for people like me to receive gifts?
  - At the foot of a mountain, a fishing village, I recall.
  - After I was struck down, washed upon their sacred shores,
  - they welcomed me as one of their own.
  - The children left marks,
  - little carvings onto my burden, a token to remember them by.
  - It was a strange feeling.
  - an unexplicable, foreign, disgusting feeling.
  - I've passed and abandoned plenty of places before.
  - The soles beneath my feet meet the dirt once more.
  - They plant into the soil, seeking purchase between every push.
  - The dirt gives way almost kindly, agonizingly so.
  - Is there any merit to such an ignorant existence?
  - That's a worm
  - Innocent.
  - Oblivious.
  - Or perhaps, it simply learned to live despite itself?
  - It's strange, this object.
  - The journal.
  - Throughout my life,
  - the burden has been the only object of significance to my being.
  - The burden is heavy,
  - at times the physical weight it imparts upon my body is unbearable.
  - But 'burden' has always been simple.
  - It is heavy, it is painful.
  - but the weight it imposes upon me is familiar.
  - Almost comforting.
  - But this journal is strange.
  - Instead, the pages are blank.
  - It does not compel me to write on it, yet I am drawn to it.
  - As if I might write something I never meant to believe.
  - Its cold.
  - Too high.
  - Does that mean I'm almost there?
  - Yet it doesn't feel any lighter.
  - The soil is too moist, the earth steep.
  - If I stop, if I rest now, I might tumble.
  - I might have to start again.
  - I don't want that.
  - Surely they can absolve me.
  - Surely, after everything, after I've proven myself.
  - Surely they can help me.
  - Maybe that sinking I feel will finally leave.
  - Maybe the weight will give way.
